<br>

## Standard ggplot2 colours   

The standard ggplot2 colours are pretty nice. To understand these see [The default colours of ggplot2](https://www.statology.org/ggplot-default-colors/). 

To view the basic colours in a chart...  


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(tidyverse)
library(ggpubr)

#create data frame
df <- data.frame(team=c('A', 'B', 'C'),
                 points=c(22, 28, 15))

#create bar plot using df
ggplot(df, aes(x=team, y=points, fill=team)) +
  geom_bar(stat = "identity")
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](2_colours_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-1-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::


<br>

To obtain the hex codes of these colours we can use the hue_pal function...  


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(scales)

#extract hex color codes for a plot with three elements in ggplot2 
hex <- hue_pal()(3)

#display hex color codes
hex
## [1] "#F8766D" "#00BA38" "#619CFF"

show_col(hex)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](2_colours_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-2-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::


<br>

To see the colours ggplot2 will use as standard with different numbers of groups... 


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
#set margins of plot area
par(mai = c(0.1, 0, 0.1, 0), bg = "grey85")

#create plot with ggplot2 default colors from 1 to 8
gc.grid <- layout(matrix(1:8, nrow = 8))
for(i in 1:8){
   gc.ramp <- hue_pal()(i)
   plot(c(0, 8), c(0,1),
        type = "n", 
        bty="n", 
        xaxt="n", 
        yaxt="n", xlab="", ylab="")
   for(j in 1:i){
      rect(j - 1, 0, j - 0.25, 1, col = gc.ramp[j])
   }
}
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](2_colours_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-3-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::


<br> 

We can the also return the hex codes associated with them. 


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
#display ggplot2 default hex color codes from 1 to 8
for(i in 1:8){
  print(hue_pal()(i))
}
```

